<p>Start address of the colour screen: one byte per cell giving the foreground
colour of the character displayed there. It has the same layout as
SCREEN_CHAR_LOC, so the cell at column x, row y is at offset
<strong>y * SCREEN_WIDTH + x</strong>.</p>
<p>The address depends on the machine, and on the VIC 20 also on the memory
expansion the project is built for, so always use the constant rather than a
literal address.</p>
<p>Write <strong>#SCREEN_COL_LOC</strong> when assigning it to a pointer: a named address
constant assigned bare can be read as an address instead of a value.</p>

<p>Example</p>
<code>
// paint the whole colour screen green
ClearScreen(GREEN, #SCREEN_COL_LOC);

// or follow screenmemory into colour memory
screenmemory := #SCREEN_CHAR_LOC + SCREEN_WIDTH * 5;
SetColorMemoryAddress();
colormemory[0] := RED;
</code>
<p>On the VIC 20 only the low nibble of each byte is used, and only the first
eight colours are available for characters unless multicolour mode is on.</p>
